#!/bin/sh
# Checks the installed packages (python3-libtmux-easy, libtmux-easy-dash)
# under the system Python: the commands, and a dashboard drawn by the bundled
# Textual. CI runs it after installing the .debs; it works locally too.
set -eu
cd /tmp
/usr/bin/python3 -c "import libtmux_easy.tmux_lib"
lte-run true
rc=0; lte-run sh -c 'exit 3' || rc=$?; test "$rc" = 3
lte-dash --help > /dev/null

# A dashboard, on a private tmux server. The library must pick lte-dash (and
# so the bundle) even with apt's too-old python3-textual installed.
TMUX_TMPDIR=$(mktemp -d /tmp/lte-smoke-XXXX)
export TMUX_TMPDIR
unset TMUX TMUX_PANE
# '|| true': the server is usually gone already, and under set -e a failing
# command in the trap would fail the script.
trap 'tmux kill-server 2>/dev/null || true; rm -rf "$TMUX_TMPDIR"' EXIT
/usr/bin/python3 - <<'PY'
import subprocess, time
from libtmux_easy import TmuxSession

s = TmuxSession.create("smoke", kill_existing=True)
s.new_window("main")
s.new_pane("worker", "sleep 60", mode="wrap")
s.dashboard(select=False)
for _ in range(100):
    text = subprocess.run(["tmux", "capture-pane", "-p", "-t", s._dash_sidebar],
                          capture_output=True, text=True).stdout
    if "worker" in text:
        break
    time.sleep(0.1)
else:
    raise SystemExit("the dashboard sidebar never listed the pane:\n" + text)
s.close_dashboard()
s.kill()
PY
echo "smoke test passed"
